  "notes": "Stress variant: FinCalC's literal 1-15 year card. 15 rows, 4 columns (a green growth column with a 2-line label), silent, whole rows unmask (frame 1 shows year 1 filled and 14 empty numbered rows), one mark that falls back to the formula bar (no room for a tooltip slot), and a formula-bar verdict. Maths: $200 at each month-end, 10% a year compounded monthly (r = 0.10/12). Worth W = 200 × ((1 + r)^(12y) − 1) / r rounded to the dollar; put in = $2,400 × y (exact); growth = W − put in rounded (put in is whole dollars, so round(W) − put in = round(W − put in)). Year 1: 2,513.11; 5: 15,487.41; 10: 40,969.00; 12: 55,287.58 (growth 26,487.58 < 28,800 put in); 13: 63,590.02 (growth 32,390.02 > 31,200: the first year growth beats what was put in); 15: 82,894.07 (growth 46,894.07)."
